00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:36.000
Rotifers don’t really get a lot of love&nbsp;
when it comes to microscopic animals.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.800 --> 00:00:39.760
At least as far as the public imagination goes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.320 --> 00:00:47.040
the rotifer is overshadowed by its fellow&nbsp;
metazoan of the microcosmos: the tardigrade.&nbsp;

00:00:53.840 --> 00:01:00.960
And we might be part of the problem. In the first&nbsp;
few months of the channel, we did spend an entire&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.960 --> 00:01:07.680
episode on our rotifer friends. But despite&nbsp;
how often they show up in our videos, spinning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.680 --> 00:01:15.920
their wheels, we have many more videos focused on&nbsp;
tardigrades…and we even made a tardigrade T-shirt.&nbsp;

00:01:16.560 --> 00:01:23.360
Of course, there is a very simple explanation&nbsp;
for this. Tardigrades are weird-looking,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.360 --> 00:01:29.440
but they’re also kind of cute. At times,&nbsp;
they may even seem kind of cuddly.&nbsp;

00:01:30.240 --> 00:01:39.840
Rotifers, on the other hand…well, they’re mostly&nbsp;
just weird. Very cool, but also very weird.&nbsp;

00:01:40.400 --> 00:01:47.600
But rotifers are making a bit of a comeback—a very&nbsp;
literal one. This year, researchers announced that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.600 --> 00:01:54.160
they were able to recover a living rotifer&nbsp;
frozen inside 24,000 year old permafrost&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.160 --> 00:02:01.200
collected from a river in northeastern Siberia.
While some species of rotifers have been noted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:07.760
for their tardigrade-like ability to withstand&nbsp;
long periods of low temperatures, scientists had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.760 --> 00:02:14.880
previously observed those periods to be more on&nbsp;
the order of 6 to 10 years. That’s still a pretty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.880 --> 00:02:22.640
impressive amount of time to survive freezing&nbsp;
conditions, but it’s a far cry from 24,000 years.&nbsp;

00:02:23.200 --> 00:02:27.760
This doesn’t mean that we should be expecting&nbsp;
ancient rotifers to be popping out of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.760 --> 00:02:33.120
ground or anything. It’s just that it’s&nbsp;
so remarkable to imagine a world where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.120 --> 00:02:40.880
tardigrades aren’t alone in their complex bodies&nbsp;
and resilient capabilities…and then to realize&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.880 --> 00:02:47.440
that world isn’t imaginary. That rotifers have&nbsp;
been right there alongside them the whole time.&nbsp;

00:02:49.120 --> 00:02:54.640
And so today, we want to take a moment&nbsp;
to appreciate the rotifer from the inside&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.640 --> 00:03:01.760
out. With many animals, that would require&nbsp;
dissection. But fortunately for us,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.760 --> 00:03:07.200
a little bit of strong light and our most&nbsp;
recent microscope updates lets us see the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.200 --> 00:03:13.280
inner machinations of the rotifer at work.
It’s almost like we’re on a field trip,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.280 --> 00:03:19.360
huddled together on some kind of magic school&nbsp;
bus as we journey through the organism itself.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.160 --> 00:03:29.600
All around us is a living exhibit&nbsp;
made up of around 1,000 cells.&nbsp;

00:03:30.320 --> 00:03:35.120
That number of cells will stay constant&nbsp;
over the course of the rotifer’s lifetime.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:40.800
But there are also about 2,400&nbsp;
known species of rotifers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.440 --> 00:03:48.560
which means there have been at least 2,400 ideas&nbsp;
of just how to put 1,000 cells to good use.&nbsp;

00:03:49.200 --> 00:03:54.960
So what we’ll be seeing today are just some&nbsp;
of those uses. Because it turns out, there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.960 --> 00:04:02.240
are many many many different ways to actually be&nbsp;
a rotifer and to actually look like a rotifer.&nbsp;

00:04:02.800 --> 00:04:07.200
But different as they may be, there are&nbsp;
a few traits that unite these strange&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.200 --> 00:04:11.760
swimming organisms—including what&nbsp;
will be the first stop on our tour.&nbsp;

00:04:12.640 --> 00:04:17.680
This is the corona, the moving,&nbsp;
hairy crown atop the rotifer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.680 --> 00:04:23.040
that gives it the name “wheel bearer.” It will&nbsp;
look different for different species, but this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.040 --> 00:04:29.440
spinning headband shares one specific purpose: to&nbsp;
stir up the water and bring food to the rotifer.&nbsp;

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:34.560
Then the food will pass on to&nbsp;
another part shared by all rotifers:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.560 --> 00:04:39.600
their jaw, also called the trophi, which&nbsp;
is settled into a muscular structure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.600 --> 00:04:46.640
called the mastax. You can see it at work here,&nbsp;
opening and closing inside the rotifer’s head&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.640 --> 00:04:49.840
as it passes food from the&nbsp;
head to the rest of its body.&nbsp;

00:04:50.800 --> 00:04:56.720
Jaws have evolved many times in the animal&nbsp;
kingdom over the past 500 million years or so,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.360 --> 00:05:01.200
which means that you can find a lot of&nbsp;
different jaws out there in the wild.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.920 --> 00:05:07.200
Even among rotifers, there are at least 9&nbsp;
different types of trophi that we know of,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.200 --> 00:05:09.840
and their shape depends on&nbsp;
the diet of the rotifer.&nbsp;

00:05:10.720 --> 00:05:16.480
And as you look around at the trophi, what&nbsp;
you’re seeing is an engineering marvel.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.120 --> 00:05:22.640
There are at least seven distinct parts to&nbsp;
it, each with their own set of actions that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.640 --> 00:05:30.560
they perform over the rotifer’s lifetime without&nbsp;
wearing down or breaking. They grab, they crush,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.560 --> 00:05:37.360
they pierce. It’s like a tiny little&nbsp;
machine inside the rotifer, which is why&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.360 --> 00:05:43.680
researchers have even looked to the trophi&nbsp;
as inspiration for future tiny little robots.&nbsp;

00:05:44.320 --> 00:05:50.880
Does that mean we can expect a rotifer&nbsp;
robot any time soon? Probably not.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.600 --> 00:05:57.440
But who needs robots when we’ve got the&nbsp;
real live thing right here in front of us?&nbsp;

00:05:58.240 --> 00:06:04.880
The trophi’s main purpose is to pass food from&nbsp;
the outside to the inside, which takes us to our&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.880 --> 00:06:12.160
next—and perhaps most perilous—stop, the stomach,&nbsp;
with its enzymes designed to break down food.&nbsp;

00:06:12.800 --> 00:06:18.240
Though…if the rotifer is male, you might not&nbsp;
actually have to watch out after all because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.800 --> 00:06:24.640
there might not be a stomach...because they&nbsp;
just don’t live long enough to need one.&nbsp;

00:06:25.520 --> 00:06:32.640
Rotifer species differ in how they approach the&nbsp;
necessity of males. There are some that have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.640 --> 00:06:38.800
done away with the concept of males entirely,&nbsp;
relying on asexual reproduction to survive.&nbsp;

00:06:39.680 --> 00:06:45.440
Others will keep them around for a short time,&nbsp;
though primarily for sexual reproduction.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.320 --> 00:06:50.880
These male rotifers are smaller,&nbsp;
and their survival lasts just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.880 --> 00:06:58.480
long enough to allow them to fertilize eggs.
So returning our attention to the female rotifer’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.480 --> 00:07:05.920
body, we can see the oocyte in the ovary, nested&nbsp;
in a tissue called the vitellarium. When food&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.920 --> 00:07:13.200
is plenty, the vitellarium can provide RNA,&nbsp;
protein, and liquid droplets to the oocyte, so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.200 --> 00:07:20.800
that it swells and swells, pushing the rest of the&nbsp;
organs around until finally the egg is released.&nbsp;

00:07:22.080 --> 00:07:30.960
The last stop on this tour de rotifer is the&nbsp;
foot, which even extends into a toe. But unlike&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.960 --> 00:07:37.760
our toes, the rotifer toe has a cement gland&nbsp;
in it, producing a glue that keeps the rotifer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.760 --> 00:07:43.920
stuck to whatever surface appeals to them.
At least, that’s the case for many rotifers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.920 --> 00:07:49.120
as they stretch out from a single anchor&nbsp;
point and put their frantic crowns to work.&nbsp;

00:07:50.240 --> 00:07:59.600
But some rotifers don’t want to be tied down. They&nbsp;
prefer a more mobile lifestyle. These planktonic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.600 --> 00:08:06.080
rotifers might end up in the open waters where&nbsp;
there isn’t much vegetation to crawl around on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.080 --> 00:08:12.880
or to stick to. So they just don’t.&nbsp;
They might forego a foot altogether.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.440 --> 00:08:19.520
Instead, they swim around, on occasion even&nbsp;
using the cilia on the crowns to help them.&nbsp;

00:08:20.240 --> 00:08:26.080
But if they bump up against a predator or&nbsp;
something else that makes them uncomfortable,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.080 --> 00:08:32.000
they can do something you might not&nbsp;
usually imagine rotifers doing. They jump.&nbsp;

00:08:33.600 --> 00:08:39.840
They can do this because they have paddles. To&nbsp;
see them, we have to return to where we started,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.560 --> 00:08:46.960
to the front of the rotifer, where these striated&nbsp;
blades make up the planktonic rotifer’s jumping&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.960 --> 00:08:54.800
apparatus. They work incredibly quickly,&nbsp;
taking only about 7 milliseconds to get going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.800 --> 00:09:01.680
if a rotifer has made contact with a predator.
As we’ve travelled through the rotifer’s body,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.680 --> 00:09:08.320
we’ve seen just how much can be made with so&nbsp;
little. And should we live in a future filled with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.320 --> 00:09:15.680
resurrected ancient rotifers and robotic modern&nbsp;
ones, then perhaps we will appreciate them—not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.880 --> 00:09:24.000
for the cute tardigrades they aren’t, but for the&nbsp;
intricate little living machines that they are.&nbsp;

00:09:31.360 --> 00:09:37.520
Thank you for coming on this journey with us as&nbsp;
we explore the unseen world that surrounds us.&nbsp;

